.TH CLOG_FLUSH 3 "2026-08-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_flush \- block until queued log records are written
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_flush(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until write(2) has accepted every record that
was queued through logger (or through a sibling handle that
shares the same target below) at the moment of this call, together
with whatever a partly filled aggregation buffer holds.

For a synchronous logger, which is one created with no clog_async_cfg_t
(see clog_open_fd_mp(3) and clog_open_file_mp(3)), this call does
nothing and returns at once.

This call does not promise to cover a record that another thread
submits at the same time as this call, or strictly after it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It is safe to call this on any open handle at any time, including at
the same time as an ordinary log_*() call on the same handle or on a
sibling handle.
On a rare allocation failure, the async queue below can fail to accept
the flush request.  This function then returns at once instead of
blocking on an acknowledgment that will never arrive.
In a forked child, a logger whose async mode has fallen back to
synchronous logging (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)) does nothing here: the
call behaves exactly as it does on a synchronous logger.
When a write stops early with an error that a later attempt can
outlast, such as ENOSPC, the bytes that it did not take stay in the
aggregation buffer and the next flush offers them again, so this call
can return while part of what it flushed is pending.  After any other
write error, such as EPIPE, the flush drops those bytes and writes a
record that names the loss.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3) for both rules and
for what they promise about the framing of a record.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3)
